<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!--

    Copyright (C) 2026 Antony Stubbs and contributors

    SpotBugs exclusions for the gRPC transport (astubbs#242; the policy is in
    docs/client-static-analysis.md). The gate itself is bound in the parent pom.

    Every entry names one class and one pattern, and mostly one method, so a NEW site of the same
    shape still fails the build. Every entry is a judgement, not a mute: if you are adding one,
    write why, or fix the code. The detector's other findings on this module were fixed rather than
    listed here - two ignored CountDownLatch.await results in SessionEndTest, which were a real hole
    (a timed-out await returned the same success the assertions expect).

-->
<FindBugsFilter>

    <!--
        THE SUGGESTED WIDENING WOULD WEAKEN A FROZEN CONTRACT, which is the same call
        astubbs/parallel-consumer#383 made for WireDurations.toJava and WireTimestamps.toJava and
        for the same reason. A DispatchRecord is a message somebody could have sent; a
        DispatchRecordOrBuilder also admits a mutable, mid-construction builder, so widening the
        parameter would let a caller obtain an InboundRecord for a value that was never on any wire.
        In the class whose entire job is "what exactly was in the message", that is the wrong
        direction to trade for a wider parameter type.
    -->
    <Match>
        <Class name="bz.stub.parallelconsumer.client.grpc.WireMapping"/>
        <Method name="toInboundRecord"/>
        <Bug pattern="OCP_OVERLY_CONCRETE_PARAMETER"/>
    </Match>

    <!--
        THE OVERWRITE THE DETECTOR FEARS CANNOT HAPPEN, and the guard that prevents it is three lines
        above the assignment: pollAsync sets `polled` under synchronized(this) and throws
        IllegalStateException on a second call, so the field is assigned at most once per client. The
        API states that at-most-once rule on both poll methods, the direct transport enforces the
        same one, and DirectParallelConsumerClientTest#pollMayBeCalledAtMostOnce pins it. Shutting
        down a pool that is never replaced would be dead code; the shutdown that does matter is in
        close(), which SpotBugs cannot connect to this assignment.
    -->
    <Match>
        <Class name="bz.stub.parallelconsumer.client.grpc.GrpcParallelConsumerClient"/>
        <Method name="pollAsync"/>
        <Bug pattern="HES_EXECUTOR_OVERWRITTEN_WITHOUT_SHUTDOWN"/>
    </Match>

    <!--
        STATIC WOULD BE A BUG, NOT A TIDY-UP. dispatchQueueDepth is the in-flight ceiling THIS
        session negotiated - it is written from the Configured reply, per client - so making it
        static would share one session's ceiling with every other client in the JVM and put a
        stranger's number in the protocol-violation message. The detector reaches this suggestion
        because the field's only write is inside an inner class; the field is per-instance by
        construction.

        Scoped to the one field's class and pattern, so any other field on it that really is a
        constant is still reported.
    -->
    <Match>
        <Class name="bz.stub.parallelconsumer.client.grpc.GrpcParallelConsumerClient"/>
        <Field name="dispatchQueueDepth"/>
        <Bug pattern="SPP_FIELD_COULD_BE_STATIC"/>
    </Match>

    <!--
        THE CONSTRAINT THE DETECTOR CANNOT SEE IS ONE FRAME UP. awaitConnected turns the three
        checked exceptions of Future#get into unchecked ones, and fb-contrib calls the method
        "unconstrained" because, being private, it could simply declare them. It could - and its only
        caller could not: pollAsync implements ParallelConsumerClient, whose signature declares no
        checked exception and is mirrored by nine other languages, several with no checked exceptions
        at all. The softening has to happen between those two frames; doing it in the caller instead
        would move the same three catch blocks into the middle of the poll path to satisfy a detector,
        without changing what any caller can observe.

        Scoped to the one method. Any OTHER method here that softens a checked exception is still
        reported, which is the case worth hearing about.
    -->
    <Match>
        <Class name="bz.stub.parallelconsumer.client.grpc.GrpcParallelConsumerClient"/>
        <Method name="awaitConnected"/>
        <Or>
            <Bug pattern="EXS_EXCEPTION_SOFTENING_NO_CONSTRAINTS"/>
            <!--
                And the lost stack trace is the same unwrap the API's own Outcomes.unwrap performs,
                for the same stated reason: an ExecutionException is the wrapper the completion
                machinery adds, not anything the proxy did. Rethrowing the ProxyProtocolViolation
                itself hands the caller the exception that was actually constructed at the violation,
                with its own stack; re-wrapping it would name the plumbing instead.
            -->
            <Bug pattern="LEST_LOST_EXCEPTION_STACK_TRACE"/>
        </Or>
    </Match>

    <!--
        Same shape, in a test fixture: FakeProxy#reportsBeforeHalfClose records how many verdicts had
        arrived when THAT proxy was half-closed, and both tests construct their own proxy. Static
        would carry one test's count into the next. Again the only write is inside an anonymous
        StreamObserver, which is what the detector is reading.
    -->
    <Match>
        <Class name="bz.stub.parallelconsumer.client.grpc.SessionEndTest$FakeProxy"/>
        <Field name="reportsBeforeHalfClose"/>
        <Bug pattern="SPP_FIELD_COULD_BE_STATIC"/>
    </Match>

</FindBugsFilter>
